pace-locally-finite-type-over-base}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:f14f2ca426a9ce85390cf65489567460594d4294cc3af4e4d6341012d81c27de", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:341b5da10c63569109c1d6803c6e8f71feb64661252217026de5020145b4f250", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:ab48158b3348537104a678652ac84b76f1c1ff2d74bd414bbfb46e873153816f", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn many situations the algebraic spaces <MATH_0001> and <MATH_0002> are locally of finite type\nover <MATH_0003> and the orbit space condition simply means that\n<MATHSEG_0001>equivalence relation generated by <MATHSEG_0002>\nfor all algebraically closed fields <MATH_0004>. See\nLemma <REF_0001>.\nIf <MATH_0005> is also a (set-theoretic) pre-equivalence relation, then the condition\nis simply equivalent to <MATH_0006> being bijective for all\nalgebraically closed fields <MATH_0007>.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:bef0f84f513312ad3bf0d2474f2ec521b878133e334880b2eb5c56d8a12171d0", "unit_id": "tag:04A1:prose-0002"}
